  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION/MARINE/BEACHES DISCUSSION...\n\nNortheast Winds will gradually weaken through the afternoon with \nlight offshore flow continuing into Friday morning, transitioning \nto a sea breeze in the afternoon. The upper level ridge moving \noverhead into Friday combined with weak offshore flow will \ngenerate warm conditions across the region. Widespread highs in \nthe low to mid 80s are expected across the coastal areas, valleys,\nand low deserts both days, warmest on Friday. Highs in the \nmountains will be in the low 60s to low 70s, and mid 60s to low \n70s in the high desert.\n\nThe upper level ridge breaks down in response to an upper low\nmoving across the top of it and into the Pac NW this weekend,\nallowing for gradual cooling and a return of onshore flow, though\nhigh temperatures remain several degrees above normal. Otherwise\njust expect some scattered to broken high clouds at times this\nweekend.\n\nPrecipitable water increases to around 0.8\" on Monday as upper\nlevel zonal flow ushers in some subtropical moisture. However,\nonly weak, very subtle short waves will be moving through the mean\nflow, providing little if any forcing for precipitation. Current\nforecast has a 25-45% chance of precipitation for the mountains\nwestward on Monday, but any accumulation will likely be limited to\none-tenth inch or less. \n\nA deep long wave trough is then forecast to move through\nCalifornia Tuesday into Wednesday, bringing gusty southwest to\nwest winds and widespread precipitation to the region. There\nremains differences in the ensembles with regards to how far south\nthis trough will dig, as well as the timing. The more significant\nspread lies in the forecast Integrated Vapor Transport (IVT). The\nGEFS and EPS 10th to 90th percentiles of IVT range from about 150\nto over 600 kg/m/s, so anywhere from too low to be considered an\nAtmospheric River to a moderate AR. This is likely the main cause\nof the large spread in forecast rainfall from this system. The NBM\n25th percentile 48 hr precipitation currently ranges from about\n1.5-2\" for the mountains westward, up to 2.5\" on the coastal\nslopes of the mountains, while the 75th percentile is around\n2.3-3\" for the mountains westward, and 3.5-4\" on the coastal\nmountain slopes. While it is far too early to nail down specific\nrainfall totals, this is leaning towards a moderate to heavy rain\nevent. Potential snow levels range from around 6500 ft in a colder\nscenario event to as high as 8000 ft on the warmer end. Weak\ninstability (surface based CAPE of 100-200 J/kg) will produce a\nslight chance of thunderstorms Tue-Wed as well, mainly over the\ncoastal waters into the valleys. Light showers could persist into\nThursday (10-20% chance).",
